    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d159">

                <pb facs="0302" n="214" sameAs="frus:frus1892/pg214"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wharton to Mr.
                            von Holleben.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1892-03-10"><hi rendition="#italic">March 10,
                        1892</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Mr. von Munini’s note of
                    November 3, 1891, submitting a proposed arrangement between the United States
                    and Germany for the reciprocal protection of patents and trade-marks, I have now
                    the honor to inclose for your information a copy of a report from F. A. Seely,
                    Chief Examiner of the United States Patent Office, which has been forwarded to
                    the Department with a letter from the Acting Secretary of the Interior, of the
                    2d instant, under his approval. It will be observed that the Acting Secretary
                    agrees to the proposed treaty with the exception of article 3, and explains why
                    that can not be accepted by this Government in its present form. In lieu of it
                    he offers a substitute. A proposition for an additional article is also made
                    with explanations as to the necessity for it.</p>
                <p>For your further information I inclose a copy of the treaty<anchor n="*"
                        xml:id="d159-f1"/> as proposed and will thank you for any information that
                    you may be able to <pb facs="0303" n="215" xml:id="pg215"/> impart as to its
                    acceptance in its present form by the Government of Germany.</p>
                <p>Upon receipt of a favorable response from you I shall lose no time in having the
                    treaty engrossed for signature.</p>
                <p>Accept, sir, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William F. Wharton</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d159-a1.xml" xpointer="d159-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d159-f1" n="*" xml:id="d159-f1-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#pg200">page 200</ref>. The treaties differ only in phraseology
                    and in the changes to article 3 recommended in the report of Mr. Seely.</p>
            </note>
